{
  "_note": "Golden values for sample_dst_log.csv. Worked out by hand. This log exists to catch two bugs that both tools have to stay clear of. Downtime is in seconds.",
  "_by_hand": {
    "why this log exists": "The browser tool used to read timestamps in the machine's local time while the Python tool read them as plain clock numbers. On a laptop set to a timezone that observes daylight saving, the two tools gave different downtime for the same log. This log spans both daylight saving changes in the United States in 2026, so a tool that gets it wrong cannot pass.",
    "E101 on CH-A": "01:30 to 03:30 on March 8, the spring change. Two hours of clock time, so 7200 s. Read as local time it would come out as 3600 s.",
    "E202 on CH-B": "01:00 to 02:30 on November 1, the autumn change. Ninety minutes of clock time, so 5400 s. Read as local time the 01:00 hour happens twice and it could come out as 9000 s.",
    "E303 on CH-C": "February 30th does not exist. The row is rejected. JavaScript used to turn it into March 2nd, which invents an alarm that never happened.",
    "E404 on CH-C": "10:00 to 11:00 on March 9, an ordinary hour away from any change. 3600 s.",
    "the window": "Set to 365 days so all three real alarms stay in it. The newest onset is the November 1 set.",
    "the ties": "All three faults have a count of one, so the count ranking is a three way tie. Both tools break it by name, so the order is E101, E202, E404."
